## 2. CPU & PARALLELISM TUNING

### Core Discovery
- Physical cores = real execution units (ignore SMT for base parallelism).
- Logical CPUs = physical cores × threads per core (nproc).

### max_worker_processes
- Set to total logical CPUs.
- Background workers (parallel, autovacuum, logical replication, etc.).

### Parallelism Budget (physical cores)
- Apply policy: parallel_workers_pct (50% conservative, 100% aggressive).
- Budgeted cores = physical_cores × parallel_workers_pct / 100.

### max_parallel_workers
- Total parallel workers across all queries = budgeted cores.
- Hard cap at 64.

### max_parallel_workers_per_gather
- Workers per query = max(1, floor(max_parallel_workers / 4)).
- Allows 1 (no parallelism) on very small systems.

### max_parallel_maintenance_workers
- Parallel workers for CREATE INDEX, VACUUM (parallel option).
- = min(8, max_parallel_workers_per_gather).

### autovacuum_max_workers
- Dedicated autovacuum background processes.
- = max(2, floor(physical_cores / 2)), capped at 10.
- Minimum 2 prevents table bloat.

### Parallel Query Cost Thresholds (prevent tiny queries going parallel)
- min_parallel_table_scan_size: 8 MB (default) – table must be at least this big.
- min_parallel_index_scan_size: 512 KB (default) – index size threshold.
- parallel_setup_cost: 1000 (default) – planner cost to start parallel workers.
- parallel_tuple_cost: 0.1 (default) – cost to transfer a tuple between workers.

### enable_parallel_append
- on by default (allows parallel append for UNION/partitioned tables).

### force_parallel_mode
- off (normal), on (testing), regress (debug) – never enable in production.

### max_slave_threads (logical replication)
- Number of logical replication apply workers per subscription.
- Default 8; increase for high‑throughput logical replication.

